Z||000000||Richard Parnell||10-26-1999||07:15 PM||||Regarding the rear struts on Fieros.  Aren't they the same thing as coil over?  If not what exactly is the difference.  I've read the different strings on this and am still SOO CONFUSED! Also, what is the word on front coil over , bad idea, to much trouble, etc?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||10-26-1999||08:44 PM||||The rear struts are coil-over design but usually when someone here refers to coil-overs they mean adjustable ones, whereby you can change the bottom spring perch position for ride hight or change the springs for ones with a different rate (stiffer or softer). Hope this clears things up.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lews84||10-26-1999||09:35 PM||||25 years ago my Formula Ford had 'coil over' Armstrong (If I remember correctly) shocks. I  couldn't afford Bilsteins. The shock had a cap at the top of the rod and threads on the lower body of the shock. A threaded ring fit the threaded body. The spring went between the top cap and the threaded ring. Turning the ring added or removed compression from the  spring. The spring and shock came off as one piece.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||10-26-1999||10:10 PM||||Coil overs,like racecars use,are great for that,where the springs,and ride height are changed often,and in a hurry,but for the street,where most everyone seems perfectly happy with whatever Eibach,or a couple of other companies offer,there's just no need.<BR>Would be like having a gold plated oil filter on your Fiero.<BR>Spend the same $ on good shocks and tires,and you'll be much further ahead.||||reg||1
